WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.483
Hi, Hannah!

2
00:00:01.483 --> 00:00:05.100
(audience claps and cheers)

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.670
Okay, I have read your book.

4
00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.770
I am obsessed with it.

5
00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.610
I literally, I have it like dog-eared

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:15.240
and annotated.

7
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.740
One quote I wanna read that I really loved from an explorer,

8
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.970
Robert Swan, that you were inspired by.

9
00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.290
And he said, "The greatest threat to our planet

10
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.087
is the belief that someone else will save it."

11
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.350
Indeed, that's one of my favorite quotes.

12
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.200 line:15%
And I think it definitely resonates with me

13
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.750 line:15%
in so many other people as well,

14
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.100 line:15%
because, I think we rely on other people

15
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.960 line:15%
to solve our world's issues.

16
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.240 line:15%
But, we don't have to wait.

17
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.430 line:15%
And for many of these issues, we don't have time to waste.

18
00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.120
So, it's so important that we all do our part

19
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.170
and also take action on these issues.

20
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.270
Well, let me ask you a business question,

21
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.080
business woman to business woman.

22
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.120
So, when you were 10 years old,

23
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.220
you wanted to save this horse farm

24
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.460
that was like taking care of rescue horses

25
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.400
and you raised the funds to help these horses

26
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.940
and you in your mind thought, "Oh, that's funny.

27
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.910
'Cause all of my peers are really into the

28
00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.130
American Girl doll, Saige,

29
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.010
who happens to ride a horse."

30
00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.350
And you thought about bringing that in.

31
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.650
Where does the business side of you come in

32
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:22.850
to start correlating how to inspire people

33
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.700
about the environment

34
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.620
through things in their everyday world?

35
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.790
Like an American Girl doll?

36
00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.246
I didn't really think about it much

37
00:01:30.246 --> 00:01:31.870
as a business at the time.

38
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.940
You know, it was just fundraising, doing my part

39
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.710
for my own community of this local horse farm.

40
00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:39.310
And, it was a movie night fundraiser,

41
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.620
so we showed the Saige movie from American Girl doll,

42
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:45.300
so we knew that if we promoted it more

43
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.180
and had something that would entice people to come,

44
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.130
we'd raise more money.

45
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.880
And so, I've been trying to incorporate things

46
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.970
kind of like that in the work that I do, you know,

47
00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.660
targeting towards your audience

48
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.870
to kind of draw people in more to my message

49
00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.740
and so that it really resonates with more people

50
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.340
and you can have a greater impact.

51
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.810
Okay, what is something that we can do on a daily basis?

52
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.310
Well, Drew, that's a great question.

53
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.850
Of course today is Earth Day

54
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.750
so there's many things you can do.

55
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.150
Of course the obvious is refusing single-use plastics

56
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.400
wherever possible.

57
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.460
Those are plastic bags, straws, cups, utensils, and bottles.

58
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.990
Even just trying to buy more secondhand

59
00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.210
reduces the carbon footprint that we have.

60
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.740
But there's also a business standpoint

61
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.100
and a legislation standpoint that we also have to address.

62
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.500
And, as individuals, oftentimes we forget

63
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.600
that we can have an impact on that.

64
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.200
Whether that's signing petitions,

65
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.960
reaching out to these different CEOs

66
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.150
or even reaching out to our Representatives

67
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.330
to support environmental legislation,

68
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.153
they all have such a great impact.

69
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.990
What is the thing you're doing with Starbucks?

70
00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.260
I'm desperate to know.

71
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.790
Yes, back in 2018, I was part

72
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.140
of the Break Free From Plastic campaign

73
00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.070
to try and really convince Starbucks to invest

74
00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.510
in a sustainable coffee cup.

75
00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.770
So we put pressure on them on social media

76
00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.700
and collected over 1 million petitions

77
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.420
and pressured them into investing in

78
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.750
new sustainable coffee cup

79
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.330
and they agreed to do so.

80
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:13.320
Okay, I just wanna thank you so much for coming here

81
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.600
and educating us on our show.

82
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.670 line:15%
And for more amazing ways that people

83
00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.020 line:15%
are changing the planet around the world,

84
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.250 line:15%
Check out our website at TheDrewBarrymoreShow.com.

85
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.900 line:15%
We're also gonna be featuring this book.

86
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.280 line:15%
I highly suggest everyone run out and get it.

87
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.130
It's a quick read, but man,

88
00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.130
is it a thoughtful thing that will stick with you.

89
00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:36.778 line:15%
(upbeat music)

